"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][31]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M31-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M31-L1 – Literature Review, Research Questions and Hypotheses<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Research Seminar and Master's Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Practice/research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, M19, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a testable research\nquestion, select literature transparently and distinguish hypotheses\nfrom exploratory conjectures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From observation to a research gap<\/h2>\n<p>A research seminar does not begin by selecting the most complex\npossible model. It begins with a problem whose existing solutions and\nlimitations need to be understood. The running synthetic teaching\nexample is forecasting the next day’s electricity consumption at small\nmonitoring stations. The fictional stations provide temperature and\nconsumption data; the course does not claim that a real field experiment\nhas taken place. One question is: Does temperature-based regression\nreduce the mean absolute forecast error compared with a simple baseline,\nincluding at previously unseen stations? This specifies the outcome,\ncomparator and target of generalisation. The question is predictive, not\ncausal: an accurate forecast does not establish that an intervention\nchanges consumption.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A reproducible literature search<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001627_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Four screening stages, from 52 initial records to six included illustrative studies; counts are invented.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four screening stages, from 52 initial\nrecords to six included illustrative studies; counts are\ninvented.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Literature selection in a synthetic review.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>First specify the databases, search period, search strings and\nselection rules. One possible search expression is\n<code>(\"energy demand\" OR \"electricity consumption\") AND (forecasting OR prediction) AND (\"external validation\" OR \"cross-site\")<\/code>.\nAdapt it to each database’s syntax where necessary and record the date,\nfilters and number of hits. After deduplication, screen titles and\nabstracts, followed by full texts. Distinguish methodological\ncontributions, external evaluations and reviews. For each included\nstudy, record its data type, forecast horizon, baseline, split, metrics,\nuncertainty and limitations. Where metrics are inconsistent, a\nstructured qualitative synthesis may be more appropriate than a pooled\nnumerical average. PRISMA provides a reporting framework for systematic\nreviews; a search list or simplified flow diagram does not automatically\nsatisfy its requirements.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>hits, duplicates, excluded_title, excluded_full <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">52<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">25<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>included <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> hits <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> duplicates <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> excluded_title <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> excluded_full<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> included <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(included)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>These figures are invented. They demonstrate only a consistent\nselection flow; no actual literature search was conducted for this\nexample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurable hypotheses and decisions<\/h2>\n<p>For each test station, define the difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_i=\\operatorname{MAE}_{\\mathrm{ref},i}-\\operatorname{MAE}_{\\mathrm{neu},i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin kWh. Positive values favour the new model. A directional hypothesis\nformulated in advance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:\\mu_d\\leq 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nversus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1:\\mu_d&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a two-sided test, the alternative would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_d\\ne 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe direction must not be changed after inspecting the data. A\npractically relevant improvement of, for example, 1 kWh should be\nspecified in addition to the statistical question. A protocol also sets\nout stopping rules, exclusion criteria, planned sensitivity analyses and\nhow unexpected observations will be assessed. Findings from exploratory\nanalysis may motivate new hypotheses, but these require independent\nconfirmation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Of 63 search hits, 13 are duplicates; another\n30 titles and 15 full texts are excluded. How many studies remain?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> 63 − 13 − 30 − 15 = 5 studies remain.\nReasons for exclusion must also be documented.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/gl-09-395054\">DFG:\nGuideline 9 – Research design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · guideline and explanation\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.prisma-statement.org/prisma-2020\">PRISMA 2020:\nStatement and overview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · overview opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M31-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M31-L2 – Research Design, Comparative Methods and Datasets<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Research Seminar and Master's Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Practice/research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, M19, M27, M28, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design fair model comparisons\nusing independent groups, predefined metrics and controlled model\nselection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is the unit of generalisation?<\/h2>\n<p>A dataset may contain thousands of daily observations despite\ncovering only a few stations. If days from the same station are randomly\nassigned to training and test sets, the model may exploit\nstation-specific information. For the question of performance at unseen\nstations (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L1<\/a>),\nstations must be separated as groups. Forecasts must also use only\ninformation available when the prediction is made: for instance,\ntomorrow’s temperature as measured afterwards is not a legitimate\nfeature, whereas a weather forecast available at the time is. Geographic\nand temporal generalisation may require separate tests. The design must\nreflect the intended deployment situation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Baselines, budgets and analysis plan<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001711_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Separate training, validation and test station groups, with no station appearing in more than one split.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Separate training, validation and test\nstation groups, with no station appearing in more than one\nsplit.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Station-level split without overlap.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A simple baseline could be a fixed mean estimated exclusively from\ntraining data. Other fair baselines include the latest available\nobservation and a seasonal reference, provided each is available at\nforecast time. Document search spaces and selection budgets for both the\nproposed method and more complex baselines. Choose hyperparameters and\npreprocessing using training and validation data only; use a sealed test\nset once for the primary evaluation. Optimising multiple models against\nthe final test set undermines its independence. An external test dataset\nfrom a different monitoring network may provide further evidence, but\ndoes not replace a description of measurement conditions and selection\nmechanisms.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>train <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;C&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>valid <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;E&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;F&#39;<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>test <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;G&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;H&#39;<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> (train <span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span> valid <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> train <span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span> test <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> valid <span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span> test)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(train <span class=\"op\">|<\/span> valid <span class=\"op\">|<\/span> test) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(train), <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(valid), <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(test))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>These eight stations are merely a minimal illustration, not an\nadequate sample size for reliable inference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement protocol and target metric<\/h2>\n<p>For station\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobserved days,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{MAE}_i=\\frac{1}{n_i}\\sum_{t=1}^{n_i}|y_{it}-\\hat y_{it}|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf all stations are weighted equally, the primary metric is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1}{m}\\sum_i\\operatorname{MAE}_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWeighting by days addresses a different question: stations with more\nobservations have greater influence. Also measure error during high\nloads, with missing features and during sensor outages. Record data\nprovenance, measurement interval, units, time zone, availability,\nrights, quality checks and the rules for missing target values. Model\nselection, sample-size planning and reporting are connected: arbitrarily\nmany data rows cannot replace a precise statement about the target\npopulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Station A has an MAE of 2 kWh over 2 days;\nstation B has an MAE of 4 kWh over 6 days. Calculate the equally\nstation-weighted and day-weighted MAE.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> Equal station weights: (2 + 4)/2 = 3 kWh;\nequal day weights: (2·2 + 6·4)/8 = 3.5 kWh.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/gl-09-395054\">DFG:\nGuideline 9 – Research design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · guideline and explanation\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://neurips.cc/public/guides/PaperChecklist\">NeurIPS:\nPaper Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sections on reproducibility, experiment design and\nuncertainty selectively reviewed<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M31-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M31-L3 – Sample-Size Planning, Uncertainty and Statistical Analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Research Seminar and Master's Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Practice/research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, M19, M27, M28, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Plan the number of independent\nstudy units and report paired effects with uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Planning around the smallest relevant effect<\/h2>\n<p>When comparing two forecasts at the same stations, the station-level\ndifference in error is the variable of interest. Suppose a pilot study\nsuggests a standard deviation of differences of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_d=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkWh. A difference of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkWh is predefined as relevant. Assuming independent, approximately\nnormally distributed differences and using a normal approximation, a\ntwo-sided test with significance level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand target power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\beta=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives approximately:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\approx\\left(\\frac{(z_{1-\\alpha/2}+z_{1-\\beta})\\sigma_d}{\\Delta}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With quantiles 1.96 and 0.842, this gives approximately 31.4, hence\nat least 32 stations under this approximation. Exact planning using a t\ndistribution, uncertainty in the pilot estimates, an allowance for\nattrition and dependencies may require a larger sample. The outcome and\ntest here are specified as two-sided; the directional hypothesis in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L1<\/a>\nwould require a different analysis plan.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Paired analysis of a teaching dataset<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001582_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Estimated mean difference 2 kWh and a 95 percent t interval from approximately 0.516 to 3.484 kWh.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Estimated mean difference 2 kWh and a 95\npercent t interval from approximately 0.516 to 3.484 kWh.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Paired estimate of a mean improvement.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Six fictional independent test stations yield MAE differences\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=(2,1,3,2,0,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkWh. The mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>d<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar d=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkWh and the sample standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_d=\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkWh. Assuming independent, normally distributed differences, the\ntwo-sided 95% t confidence interval is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>d<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>±<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>0.975<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mfrac><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar d\\pm t_{0.975,n-1}\\frac{s_d}{\\sqrt n}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With five degrees of freedom and the quantile 2.571, the interval is\napproximately [0.516, 3.484] kWh. Zero lies outside this interval, but\nthe practically relevant value 1 lies inside. The interval therefore\ndoes not establish that the improvement exceeds 1 kWh. The six stations\nare not outcomes of the experiment planned in S1; they constitute a\nsmall numerical example.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> t, norm<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(d)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mean <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> d.mean()<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> sd <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> d.std(ddof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> t.ppf(<span class=\"fl\">.975<\/span>, n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sd<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(n)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>plan <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(np.ceil(((norm.ppf(<span class=\"fl\">.975<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>norm.ppf(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(sd, np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> plan <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">32<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([mean<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h, mean<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h], [<span class=\"fl\">.5158739<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">3.4841261<\/span>], atol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.002<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(plan, mean, mean<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h, mean<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dependence, multiple tests and interpretation<\/h2>\n<p>Daily observations from one station must not be treated as\nindependent station-level replications. If entire regions are jointly\naffected by weather events, even a station-level test may be too\noptimistic. Possible remedies include an appropriate hierarchical model\nor resampling at the level of independent clusters, with assumptions\nchecked in either case. Multiple endpoints and model variants require a\nmultiple-comparison plan (M6); exploratory findings must remain clearly\nidentified. An interval containing zero does not prove equivalence, and\na small p-value alone says nothing about effect size or practical value.\nReport the effect estimate, uncertainty, sampling unit and deviations\nfrom the protocol.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> How does the normal-approximation sample size\nfrom S1 change if the smallest relevant difference increases to 2\nkWh?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> The ratio is halved, so its square becomes\none quarter; approximately 7.85, rounded up to 8 stations under this\napproximation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section3/prc312.htm\">NIST:\nConfidence intervals for differences between means, §7.3.1.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nformula for paired differences reviewed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://neurips.cc/public/guides/PaperChecklist\">NeurIPS:\nPaper Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sections on reproducibility, experiment design and\nuncertainty selectively reviewed<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M31-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M31-L4 – Reproducibility, Ablation and Debugging<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Research Seminar and Master's Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Practice/research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, M19, M27, M28, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Analyse a controlled ablation\nstudy and distinguish technical reproducibility from causal claims.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A result must be verifiable<\/h2>\n<p>A single successful training run provides little evidence that a\nresult can be reproduced. Record the dataset version, code revision,\nconfiguration, preprocessing, library versions, hardware and random\nsources. A deterministic seed alone does not guarantee identical results\nfor all parallel or hardware-dependent operations. Before running the\nexperiment, specify whether bit-for-bit reproducibility is required or\nwhether a scientifically justified numerical tolerance is sufficient.\nDocument negative findings and failed attempts as well; research data\nsubject to access restrictions must not simply be published. A synthetic\nsubstitute dataset can demonstrate that software runs, but not validate\nthe originally reported empirical results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ablation as a targeted model comparison<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001503_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Four model variants with mean absolute error between 5.0 and 3.2 kWh; lower is better.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four model variants with mean absolute\nerror between 5.0 and 3.2 kWh; lower is better.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Controlled feature-group ablation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Suppose a model combines temperature and calendar features. In an\nillustrative ablation, four variants are trained under the same\nsplitting, selection and evaluation rules, and assessed at the same\nstations. The following aggregate MAE values are invented for\ndemonstration, not observed results.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Features<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">MAE in kWh<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No additional features<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature only<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calendar only<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature and calendar<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The improvement due to temperature is 1.3 kWh without calendar\nfeatures and 1.4 kWh with them. The additional joint benefit relative to\nthe additive model can be expressed as the difference between these\nimprovements: 0.1 kWh. Without an uncertainty analysis, this is not\nrobust evidence of an interaction. Moreover, the comparison would not be\nproperly controlled if model capacities or budgets differed\nsubstantially.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>, (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<span class=\"fl\">3.7<\/span>, (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<span class=\"fl\">4.6<\/span>, (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<span class=\"fl\">3.2<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> v[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>v[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> v[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>v[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>((b<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a,b,b<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Debugging with counterchecks<\/h2>\n<p>Begin with a small dataset for which the expected output can be\ncalculated by hand. Check data ordering, the mapping of target values,\nunits and forecast horizon. A model that still performs unusually well\non an independent test after its training targets have been randomly\npermuted may indicate leakage or an evaluation error; this observation\nalone does not establish the cause. Compare training and validation\nbehaviour, log modifications and reproduce a bug with an automated test\nbefore applying a patch. A bug report includes expected and observed\nbehaviour, minimal input, environment and a control experiment. Confirm\na correction by rerunning the relevant test, while avoiding optimisation\nagainst the actual test set.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Suppose the full model has an MAE of 3.4\nrather than 3.2 kWh. What is the additional joint benefit relative to\nadditivity?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> Temperature with calendar: 4.6 − 3.4 =\n1.2. Without calendar: 1.3; the difference is −0.1 kWh.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://neurips.cc/public/guides/PaperChecklist\">NeurIPS:\nPaper Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sections on reproducibility, experiment design and\nuncertainty selectively reviewed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/gl-12-395172\">DFG:\nGuideline 12 – Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · guideline and explanation opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M31-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M31-L5 – Project Work with a Documented Data and Model Pipeline<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Research Seminar and Master's Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Practice/research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, M19, M27, M28, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Create a small, independently\nexecutable research artefact with a fixed split, baseline model and\nreproducible results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From protocol to research artefact<\/h2>\n<p>The following project is a minimal synthetic example based on <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L4<\/a>.\nIt compares linear regression with a constant baseline. Input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a fictional temperature, in degrees Celsius, known at forecast time;\ntarget\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis daily consumption in kWh. In a real project, the measurement\nprotocol, data licence, temperature forecasts and deployment conditions\nwould need separate examination. Define a project structure with\nimmutable raw data, a version-controlled processing script, results in a\nseparate folder and a README containing installation and execution\ninstructions. The analysis must not rely on hidden manual steps. The\nembedded data are sufficient to reproduce the entire teaching run.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Executable minimal experiment<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001637_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Reference mean absolute errors of 0 and 5 kWh compared with regression errors of 1 kWh at both stations.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Reference mean absolute errors of 0 and 5\nkWh compared with regression errors of 1 kWh at both\nstations.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two forecasting methods across test stations.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Two training stations, A and B, each supply three days; two\nindependent test stations, C and D, each supply two days. The models are\nfixed in advance, so no hyperparameters are selected and no validation\ngroup is used in this minimal example. Actual model development would\nrequire one, as described in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L2<\/a>.\nBoth the fitted model and the baseline mean use training values\nexclusively.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x_train <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y_train <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">10.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">14.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">16.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">18.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">20.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x_test <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y_test <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">15.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">15.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">19.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">21.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>group_train <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>group_test <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"st\">&#39;C&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;C&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>(group_train)<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span><span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>(group_test)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.column_stack([np.ones(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(x_train)),x_train])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>beta <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linalg.lstsq(X,y_train,rcond<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pred <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> beta[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>beta[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x_test<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>baseline <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.full(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(x_test), y_train.mean())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> station_mae(pred):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.array([np.mean(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(y_test[group_test<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>g]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>pred[group_test<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>g]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                     <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> g <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> np.unique(group_test)])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m_ref, m_new <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> station_mae(baseline), station_mae(pred)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(beta,[<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(m_ref,[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(m_new,[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(beta, m_ref, m_new, (m_ref<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m_new).mean())<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=10+2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the baseline is 15 kWh. The equally station-weighted MAEs are 2.5\nand 1 kWh, respectively; the mean paired improvement is 1.5 kWh. At\nstation C, the new method performs worse (−1 kWh improvement); at D, it\nperforms better (+4 kWh). Reporting only the overall mean conceals this\nheterogeneity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Quality assurance and handover<\/h2>\n<p>Use a development phase and a frozen evaluation phase. In a real\nproject, the dataset version, configuration, result files and their\nprovenance must be uniquely traceable. The README specifies the Python\nand NumPy versions, or a reproducible environment, the execution command\nand expected outputs. A reviewer should be able to trace inputs and\noutputs without further questions; store any new run separately from the\noriginal results. The two test stations in this teaching example are\ninsufficient for a reliable statement about the target population. The\nlinear relationship in the training data is also deliberately\nconstructed. A project report must describe these limitations and must\nnot present this teaching result as field validation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> The new model has MAEs of 1 and 2 kWh at C and\nD. The baseline is unchanged. What is the mean equally station-weighted\nimprovement?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> The differences are −1 and +3 kWh; their\nmean is 1 kWh.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://neurips.cc/public/guides/PaperChecklist\">NeurIPS:\nPaper Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sections on reproducibility, experiment design and\nuncertainty selectively reviewed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/gl-12-395172\">DFG:\nGuideline 12 – Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · guideline and explanation opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M31-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M31-L6 – Research Reports, Peer Review and Scientific Presentations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Research Seminar and Master's Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Practice/research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, M19, M27, M28, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Turn results, evidence and\nlimitations into a verifiable research report and a comprehensible\npresentation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An argument rather than a collection of results<\/h2>\n<p>A research report first explains the problem and the gap in existing\nresearch. The research question, study design, data basis and methods\nthen follow. Separate results from their interpretation: the observed\ndifference is a result; its possible origin or transferability belongs\nin the discussion. Tables and figures must make the units, unit of\nanalysis, sample size, direction of improvement and, where applicable,\nthe representation of uncertainty clear. Relevant counterfindings belong\nalongside favourable results. The miniature experiment from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L5<\/a>\ndemonstrates traceability, but cannot support reliable claims about a\npopulation of real stations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An example of verifiable reporting<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001577_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Station-averaged mean absolute error of 2.5 kWh for the reference and 1.0 kWh for regression; not field validation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Station-averaged mean absolute error of\n2.5 kWh for the reference and 1.0 kWh for regression; not field\nvalidation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Main result of a synthetic project.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For the teaching run, the equally station-weighted MAEs of the\nbaseline and regression are 2.5 and 1 kWh. The absolute difference is\n1.5 kWh, or 60% relative to the baseline. This relative measure refers\nto this particular error aggregate, not to a reduction in electricity\nconsumption; if the baseline error were zero, relative improvement would\nbe undefined. The station-specific differences are −1 and +4 kWh. A\nresponsible statement is: In this minimal synthetic experiment, the\nregression had a lower mean error, although its error was larger at one\nof the two test stations. No claim about real monitoring networks is\nestablished by this result.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ref,new <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">2.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>abs_gain <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> ref<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>new<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> rel_gain <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> abs_gain<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>ref<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> abs_gain <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> rel_gain <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(abs_gain,rel_gain)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For an empirical result with an adequate sample, predefined\nuncertainty analyses and sensitivity checks would be added. The two\nstations in this teaching run must not be combined with the six stations\nin the separate interval calculation in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Peer review and presentation<\/h2>\n<p>Peer review examines whether claims, data, methods and conclusions\nfit together. Review comments identify specific passages, justify\ncriticisms and distinguish essential errors from optional extensions.\nPossible responses are to correct an error and recompute the result,\nprovide a reasoned rebuttal or disclose a limitation that cannot be\nresolved. In a response log, connect every comment to a change, evidence\nand location. Confidential material received for review must not be\nshared without permission, and conflicts of interest must be disclosed.\nA brief presentation may cover the question, design, one main figure,\nthe most important limitation and the next research question.\nComputational artefacts, sources and detailed methods should remain\navailable for the subsequent discussion. A persuasive figure is not a\nsubstitute for methodological scrutiny.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> For a baseline error of 4 kWh and a model\nerror of 3.5 kWh, what are the absolute and relative improvements?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> 0.5 kWh and 0.5/4 = 12.5%, respectively.\nThe percentage describes the error, not energy savings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://neurips.cc/public/guides/PaperChecklist\">NeurIPS:\nPaper Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sections on reproducibility, experiment design and\nuncertainty selectively reviewed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/code\">DFG:\nCode of Conduct – Overview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · overview of the guidelines opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M31-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M31-L7 – Master's Thesis Planning, Limitations and Open Research Questions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Research Seminar and Master's Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Practice/research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, M19, M27, M28, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Plan a realistic Master’s thesis\nand translate the scope of the results into concrete follow-up\nstudies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Defining a bounded project<\/h2>\n<p>A Master’s thesis does not have to solve every open problem in AI. It\nneeds a precise question, a suitable method, realistic data access and a\ndocumented evaluation plan. The teaching example could become a proposal\nexamining whether temperature-based forecasts are more robust than\nseveral simple baselines at new stations. Before formal registration,\nclarify with the supervisor which examination regulations apply, which\ndata may be used, the actual registration, preparation and submission\ndeadlines, and available supervision. The plan proposed here is an\noriginal teaching example, not a binding university rule. State a\nminimum research question that can be completed even if findings are\nnegative, with optional extensions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dependencies, contingency and decision points<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001636_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A sequential six-phase project schedule totaling 15 weeks, including a one-week buffer.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A sequential six-phase project schedule\ntotaling 15 weeks, including a one-week buffer.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Example thesis work schedule.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A fictional plan allocates two weeks to literature and protocol,\nthree to data access and preparation, four to modelling, three to\nevaluation, two to reporting and one as contingency. Literature work may\npartially overlap with timely clarification of data access; protected\ndata must not be processed without the necessary legal authorisation. In\nthe simple sequential scenario, the total is 15 weeks. Set decision\npoints: Are data available and of sufficient quality? Can the baseline\nrun? Is the analysis plan frozen? Are results reproducible? Have report\nand artefact checks passed? If access is still unresolved after three\nweeks, the scope may be adjusted in consultation with the supervisor or\na permitted alternative dataset investigated. Any change in the target\npopulation must be documented.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>weeks <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(weeks)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">15<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>remaining_without_buffer <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(weeks[:<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> remaining_without_buffer<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">14<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(weeks),remaining_without_buffer)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations as starting points for new research<\/h2>\n<p>Internal validity concerns the reliability of the comparison under\nthe conditions studied; external validity concerns transferability to\nother stations, regions or periods. Construct validity asks whether, for\nexample, MAE adequately captures the operational objective of interest.\nSample size, measurement error, outages, selection bias and model choice\nall affect these aspects. Pair each important limitation with a possible\ntest: few stations → a predefined independent sample; homogeneous region\n→ external validation in other regions; uncertain weather forecast →\ncomparison across forecast qualities; limited error metric → additional\nevaluation under high loads. Neither a high test score nor successful\nimplementation resolves such questions. Depending on the project, a\ncomplete thesis includes a transparent protocol, a methodological\naccount, results and error analysis, documented artefacts and a\njustified discussion including negative findings. Only then does the\nnext research cycle begin.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Data preparation takes two weeks longer, while\nthe rest of the sequential plan stays unchanged. How long does the work\ntake if the contingency is used up completely?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> 14 weeks of core work + 2 weeks of delay =\n16 weeks, one week beyond the originally planned total of 15 weeks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/gl-09-395054\">DFG:\nGuideline 9 – Research design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · guideline and explanation\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/gl-12-395172\">DFG:\nGuideline 12 – Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · guideline and explanation opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/en/code\">DFG:\nCode of Conduct – Overview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · overview of the guidelines opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
